Monitor an OpenShift Cluster
Objective
Learn how to monitor the cluster using the OpenShift Web Console panels:
•	Events
•	Compute
•	Observe
These panels help administrators check:
•	cluster health
•	node status
•	application performance
•	alerts and logs
________________________________________
1. Nodes, Machines, and Machine Configuration
What is a Node
In Kubernetes, a Node is a machine where containers run.
A node can be:
•	physical server (bare metal)
•	virtual machine
•	cloud instance
Nodes run:
•	pods
•	container runtime
•	kubelet
•	networking services
When you deploy an application:
Pod → scheduled → Node
Example command:
oc get nodes
Output example:
NAME        STATUS   ROLES
master-1    Ready    master
worker-1    Ready    worker
________________________________________
Machine vs Node in OpenShift
In normal Kubernetes:
Machine ≈ Node
But in OpenShift, a Machine is a resource that represents the node.
Example:
Machine resource
       ↓
Creates
       ↓
Node
Machine resources are useful for cloud infrastructure automation.
Example command:
oc get machines
________________________________________
2. MachineConfig and Machine Config Operator (MCO)
MachineConfig
A MachineConfig defines how cluster nodes should be configured.
It controls:
•	operating system configuration
•	system files
•	services
•	updates
•	kubelet version
•	CRI-O version
Example command:
oc get machineconfig
________________________________________
Machine Config Operator (MCO)
The Machine Config Operator ensures that nodes follow the MachineConfig.
Responsibilities:
•	maintain node configuration
•	apply OS updates
•	update system services
•	enforce cluster configuration
Example flow:
Admin changes MachineConfig
        ↓
MCO detects change
        ↓
Updates nodes
        ↓
Nodes restart if required
________________________________________
Node Update Order
OpenShift updates nodes zone-wise using label:
topology.kubernetes.io/zone
This prevents the whole cluster from restarting simultaneously.
