4. Viewing Node Logs
Node logs help troubleshoot problems.
Example issues:
•	node not ready
•	kubelet failure
•	container runtime failure
From the console:
Compute → Nodes → Node Name → Logs
CLI equivalent:
oc adm node-logs <node-name>
________________________________________
5. Debugging Nodes with Terminal
The web console provides a terminal to access the node.
This terminal connects through a debug pod.
A debug pod is a temporary container used to access node resources.
Example command (CLI):
oc debug node/<node-name>
Example:
oc debug node/worker-1
This creates a debug container on the node.
Inside the debug environment you can run system commands.
________________________________________
Important Rule
Directly modifying nodes is not recommended.
Instead:
•	update MachineConfig
•	let MCO apply the changes
________________________________________
6. Accessing Pod Logs
Administrators often check pod logs for troubleshooting.
Menu:
Workloads → Pods
This page lists:
•	all pods
•	their status
•	node location
•	restart count
________________________________________
Viewing Pod Details
Click a pod name.
You can view:
•	logs
•	metrics
•	environment variables
•	terminal
•	YAML configuration
•	events
________________________________________
Pod Logs
Pod logs show:
•	application output
•	errors
•	warnings
CLI command:
oc logs <pod-name>
Example:
oc logs nginx-pod
________________________________________
Pod Terminal
The Terminal tab allows you to open a shell inside the container.
Example CLI equivalent:
oc exec -it <pod-name> -- /bin/bash
Example:
oc exec -it nginx-pod -- /bin/bash
This allows you to:
•	inspect files
•	test application
•	debug runtime problems
